-- 188 — the city hub carries a written introduction, not a one-line label
--
-- The ten root city pages (https://zaarhub.com/{city}) are the entry points Google actually
-- crawls, and until now their only prose was a generated sentence built from a listing count
-- ("315 local businesses in Apopka, FL, with ratings, addresses and contact details."). A page
-- whose entire body is a template plus the city name is thin content: measured 2026-11-12, the
-- served HTML carried 1,822 characters of visible text (script/style stripped, one space per
-- whitespace run), which is not enough to rank for a commercial local query and reads like a
-- doorway page to a human.
--
-- `intro_copy` holds the hand-written, city-specific prose authored by zaarhub-ceo (sha256
-- bf95d2527a0e4e87adc2d96a0b9920c6bd63548233ea0bb2f9fbc6c8b704f906, 10/10 slugs, 152-176
-- words each, no duplicate sentences, factual geography only). The renderer prints it paragraph
-- by paragraph (blank line = paragraph break) directly under the city heading, so it is in the
-- HTML served to a crawler with JavaScript disabled.
--
-- `description` is deliberately NOT touched: it is the 158-character meta description and must
-- keep the value the page already published. Nothing here edits meta, canonical or robots.
--
-- Idempotent: the column is added only if missing and every UPDATE is keyed on the city slug,
-- so re-running the file changes nothing.

ALTER TABLE city_pages ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS intro_copy TEXT;

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Apopka sits in northwest Orange County, where the Orlando metro gives way to ferneries, nurseries and open water. Lake Apopka, the state's fourth-largest lake, anchors the western edge of the city, and Wekiwa Springs State Park and Rock Springs Run draw paddlers and hikers from across Central Florida. The city has long carried the nickname "Indoor Foliage Capital of the World" because of the concentration of plant growers in the surrounding countryside, and that agricultural identity still shapes the local economy. Residents live in established neighborhoods around downtown and along the U.S. 441 corridor, with newer development pushing north toward the Wekiva Parkway. Families here care about school zones, commuter routes into Orlando, and the balance between growth and the rural buffer the city has fought to protect. The ZaarHub Apopka directory is built for that everyday decision-making: finding a dentist who takes your insurance, a mechanic who knows a fifteen-year-old truck, a landscaper, a roofer, a pediatric clinic, or a weekend breakfast spot — all organized by category and by how close they are to you.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'apopka';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Boca Raton stretches from the Atlantic beaches west across Palm Beach County to the edge of the Everglades conservation area, and it is a city defined by planning. The original 1920s plan by Addison Mizner gave it a Mediterranean architectural identity that still governs downtown, and the redeveloped Mizner Park now anchors a walkable core of shops, restaurants and cultural venues. Red Reef Park and Spanish River Park preserve public beach access, while Florida Atlantic University and a cluster of corporate offices along the I-95 corridor give the city an unusually educated, professional resident base. Neighborhoods range from oceanfront condominiums to gated country-club communities to older single-family blocks west of the interstate, and each has its own service geography. The ZaarHub Boca Raton directory organizes local businesses by category and area — medical and dental practices, home services, restaurants, financial and legal offices, fitness studios and retail — so residents can find a nearby provider with a real local reputation instead of starting from a search engine.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'boca-raton';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Hollywood runs from the Atlantic surf line west to the edge of the Everglades, one of the few Broward County cities that touches both. The Hollywood Beach Broadwalk is the city's signature — a brick promenade along the sand lined with cafés, bike rentals and small hotels — but inland Hollywood has a separate life of its own around downtown's ArtsPark, Young Circle and the historic districts laid out in the 1920s by city founder Joseph Young. Between the beach and downtown sit dense residential neighborhoods where year-round residents, snowbirds and young families share the same blocks. That mix makes the local service economy unusually broad: you have seasonal demand at the beach, steady demand in the neighborhoods, and a large multilingual business community serving both. The ZaarHub Hollywood directory organizes all of it by category, from restaurants and auto repair to contractors, salons, medical offices and professional services, so residents and visitors can find a business that is nearby, open now, and reviewed by people who actually live here.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'hollywood';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Lake Nona is a planned community in southeast Orange County, built around Lake Nona Medical City and the cluster of hospitals, research institutes and health-sciences employers that grew up there. Lake Nona Town Center provides a walkable core of restaurants, shops and entertainment, and the surrounding villages — Laureate Park, Northlake Park, Storey Park and others — each have their own parks, schools and neighborhood retail. The area's proximity to Orlando International Airport and the Beachline Expressway makes it a base for pilots, healthcare workers, engineers and remote professionals, and the housing stock skews new, with a large share of residents who arrived within the last few years. That recency is exactly why a good directory matters: newcomers have no inherited recommendations for a pediatrician, a dentist, a dog groomer, a pool service or a Saturday brunch spot. The ZaarHub Lake Nona directory supplies that layer of local knowledge, organized by category and by village, so residents can find a nearby business that other people in the community actually use.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'lake-nona';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Palm Bay is the largest city in Brevard County by land area, spread across the Space Coast south of Melbourne and west of the Indian River Lagoon. It is a city of subdivisions and long east-west corridors rather than a single dense downtown, which means where you live in Palm Bay determines which shops, schools and services are actually convenient. Turkey Creek threads through the southern part of the city and gives residents a quiet paddling and walking sanctuary, while the I-95 interchange and the nearby high-tech corridor around Melbourne and the Space Coast support a workforce tied to aerospace, defense and manufacturing. Growth has been steady for two decades, bringing new retail centers, medical offices and charter schools alongside the older neighborhoods. Because the city is so spread out, a good local directory matters here more than almost anywhere: the ZaarHub Palm Bay directory lists businesses by category and neighborhood so residents can find a nearby plumber, HVAC company, dentist, mechanic, landscaper or restaurant without driving across three ZIP codes to get there.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'palm-bay';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Palm Coast occupies most of Flagler County, stretched between the Intracoastal Waterway and the Atlantic along a coastline of coquina beaches and maritime hammocks. The city was master-planned in the 1960s and grew in phases of numbered subdivisions, which gives it a distinctive geography: quiet residential "sections" connected by parkway corridors, with commercial centers clustered along Palm Coast Parkway and State Road 100. Washington Oaks Gardens State Park south of town is known for its coquina rock shoreline and formal gardens, and the nearby Hammock area preserves some of the oldest live oak canopy in the state. Residents skew toward retirees, remote workers and families drawn by lower density and beach access, and the local economy reflects that — healthcare, home services, marine trades, dining and small retail. The ZaarHub Palm Coast directory covers those categories in one place, so a new resident can find a primary care office, a boat mechanic, a roofer after storm season, or a dependable restaurant without relying on guesswork or drive-by discovery.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'palm-coast';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Pompano Beach sits on the Atlantic in northern Broward County, with the Intracoastal Waterway and Hillsboro Inlet splitting the city between its beachside and mainland halves. It began as a farming and fishing settlement — the name comes from the pompano that filled local waters — and working waterfront culture still runs through the marinas, docks and seafood businesses along the inlet. The beachside has been redeveloped around a rebuilt pier and promenade, while the mainland side along Atlantic Boulevard and Sample Road holds most of the city's retail, light industry and dense residential blocks. That split shapes how residents shop: beachside is seasonal and hospitality-driven, mainland is year-round and service-driven. The ZaarHub Pompano Beach directory covers both, listing restaurants, marine services, contractors, auto shops, medical practices, salons and professional offices by category and neighborhood, so residents can find a business that is actually close to them rather than one that merely ranks well.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'pompano-beach';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$St. Cloud sits in northern Osceola County on the shore of East Lake Tohopekaliga, a short drive south of the Orlando airport corridor. The city grew from a late-nineteenth-century retirement colony and still keeps a compact historic downtown near the lakefront, with a park and marina where residents walk, fish and launch boats. Around that core, St. Cloud has expanded rapidly along the U.S. 192 and Narcoossee Road corridors, absorbing families priced out of Orlando and Kissimmee and adding new schools, retail centers and medical offices. That growth creates a local economy in transition: longtime family businesses downtown alongside newer chains and independent start-ups on the edges. Residents here care about commute routes, school assignments and storm-season preparedness as much as restaurants and shopping. The ZaarHub St. Cloud directory lists businesses by category and neighborhood — contractors, auto and marine repair, dental and medical offices, restaurants, childcare, landscaping and professional services — so households can find a dependable local provider without a long drive.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'st-cloud';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$St. Petersburg occupies the southern half of Pinellas County, wrapped by Tampa Bay on one side and the Gulf beaches on the other. Its downtown has become one of the most walkable in Florida, anchored by the St. Pete Pier, the Salvador Dalí Museum and a waterfront park system that runs for miles. Around that core sit distinct districts with their own character — the Grand Central District's small storefronts, the Edge District's breweries and workshops, and the historic neighborhoods of Old Northeast and Kenwood — plus the beaches of St. Pete Beach and Treasure Island just across the sand barrier. The city's economy mixes tourism, healthcare, marine and financial services with a dense small-business layer of restaurants, galleries, studios and trades. Because so much of daily life happens at the neighborhood level, the ZaarHub St. Petersburg directory is organized by category and area, letting residents find a nearby dentist, bike shop, contractor, taqueria or veterinary clinic without sorting through national chains and out-of-area listings.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'st-petersburg';

UPDATE city_pages SET intro_copy = $intro$Winter Garden anchors west Orange County at the edge of Lake Apopka, and its historic downtown on Plant Street has become one of the most visited small-city centers in Central Florida. A restored 1930s downtown, a weekly farmers market, the West Orange Trail running through the middle of it, and the former citrus-belt railroad corridor all converge within a few blocks. The city grew from citrus packing and agriculture, and the surrounding area still holds working groves, nurseries and the lake's restored marshland. Residential neighborhoods spread north and west toward Oakland and the Turnpike, mixing long-established families with newcomers drawn by the downtown and the trail. That combination — a weekend destination downtown with quiet residential streets behind it — makes local discovery uneven: visitors find the obvious spots, residents need the practical ones. The ZaarHub Winter Garden directory organizes businesses by category and neighborhood, covering contractors, dental and medical practices, restaurants, auto repair, landscaping, fitness studios and professional services, so residents can find a nearby provider they can rely on week after week.$intro$ WHERE city_slug = 'winter-garden';
